          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have coronary artery disease.",
          "answer": "Coronary artery disease is a condition where plaque builds up in the arteries that supply blood to the heart muscle. This plaque develops over many years, starting with damage to the artery lining and the accumulation of cholesterol-rich material. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Coronary artery disease\")\n\nThis plaque buildup can lead to health problems in two main ways:\n*   **Gradual narrowing:** The plaque can slowly narrow the arteries, limiting blood flow when the heart needs more, such as during exercise. This can cause predictable chest discomfort called stable angina.\n*   **Plaque rupture:** The plaque can suddenly rupture, exposing material that triggers a blood clot. This clot can block the artery, leading to an acute coronary syndrome, such as a heart attack. This can happen even with plaques that were not previously narrow enough to cause symptoms. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Coronary artery disease\")\n\nFor those diagnosed with coronary artery disease, secondary prevention measures are used to reduce the chance of future heart-related events. (Source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis\")\n\nIn medical classification, ischemic heart disease, which includes coronary artery disease, is coded in ICD-10 from I20 to I25. (Source: US clinical modification of ICD-10, \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system (I00-I99)\")\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
