          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which parts of that are lifestyle rather than medication?",
          "answer": "Lifestyle interventions are changes to daily habits and behaviors that can improve health, while medication involves taking prescribed drugs. Both are important parts of secondary prevention for coronary artery disease.\n\nHere's how to categorize some common actions:\n\n**Lifestyle Interventions:**\n*   **Dietary changes:** Reducing dietary sodium, adopting a diet rich in vegetables, fruits, whole grains, and low-fat dairy, and low in saturated fat. (Source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\")\n*   **Physical activity:** Engaging in regular aerobic activity most days of the week. (Source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\")\n*   **Weight management:** Reducing weight if it is above the healthy range. (Source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\")\n*   **Alcohol consumption:** Limiting alcohol intake. (Source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\")\n*   **Smoking cessation:** Stopping smoking. (Source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\")\n\n**Medication:**\n*   **Antiplatelet therapy:** This involves taking medicines to prevent blood clots. (Source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis\")\n*   **Statin therapy:** This involves taking medications, often high-intensity statins, to manage cholesterol levels. (Source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis\"; Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\")\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, condition-coronary-artery-disease, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation, condition-hypertension, guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment]",
